{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ וגו׳</b>. One needs to know where they were commanded with a condition regarding hearing at the beginning of the parashah, so that it would be justified to state the doubled condition ״וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ״, for He should have needed to begin only from ״וְאִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תִּמְאָסוּ״. And it appears that here the verse intends to alert you that what He said at the beginning of the matter — ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי״ — its intent is regarding the matter of Torah, and therefore He designated the negation of hearing in its opposite form. And which commandment depends on the hearing of the ear? That is the matter of Torah, in the manner of His saying (Isaiah 55:3) ״שִׁמְעוּ וּתְחִי נַפְשְׁכֶם״, ״יִשְׁמַע חָכָם וְיוֹסֶף לֶקַח״ (Proverbs 1:5). And since HaShem intended in the statement ״בְּחֻקֹּתַי״ all that we explained concerning it, in the doubled condition He suspended the entire continuation of evil upon the cause of the absence of Torah." "<b>Further</b>, since HaShem wished to state the negation of observing the commandments and refusal to do His will, may He be blessed, therefore He prefaced the cause and said that the cause of the matter is that they did not study Torah, in the manner of their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Kiddushin 30b): The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I created for you the evil inclination, I created for it the Torah as an antidote. Behold, there is no hope of escaping the evil inclination through involvement in anything other than Torah, and when he is deprived of Torah he will incline his ear to his evil inclination:" "<b>Further</b>, He intended to awaken the sleeping heart, for all who walk in a way that is not good have surely been deprived of knowledge of the Creator, blessed be He, and His goodnesses and what flows from His service — for certainly if a person were to attain one portion of the sweetness of intimacy, of pleasantness, of the sap of one branch among the branches of supernal light, he would despise in his heart being a king of kings in this world in comparison to the fulfillment of a single commandment. And this is what HaShem alerted in saying <b>וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ לִי</b> — meaning, the language of understanding; then, that is what I fear for you, that you will not do, etc.; but if you understand and know Me, there is nothing to fear. And it is the statement of the prophet (Isaiah 5:13) ״לָכֵן גָּלָה עַמִּי מִבְּלִי דָעַת״." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to alert further to His saying <b>וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ אֶת כָּל</b>, according to what I explained in the verse ״אִם בְּחֻקֹּתַי תֵּלֵכוּ״ etc. in one of the ways, that He intends to say that through involvement in Torah one will be secure from his evil inclination, for the Torah protects and saves (Sotah 21a). Therefore He said in the doubled condition ״וְאִם לֹא תִשְׁמְעוּ לִי״ — meaning, ׳to Me׳: for My name. For even if they study Torah, if the study is not לִשְׁמוֹ, for His name, may He be blessed — ״וְלֹא תַעֲשׂוּ אֵת כָּל הַמִּצְוֹת הָאֵלֶּה״, meaning, it will not save from the hand of the evil inclination, and transgressions will come upon them, for nothing saves from the evil inclination except Torah לִשְׁמָהּ, for its own sake." "<b>Further</b>, it intends that if they do not listen to the Torah, even if they agree to do all the commandments, that is of the realm of impossibility, for one cannot attain knowledge of the precise details of the commandments and their particulars, and one will not be prevented from stumbling. Also, in the manner of their statement, may their memory be for blessing (Menachot 110a) ״זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה לָעוֹלָה״ etc. (Leviticus 6:2), that one who engages in Torah, even at the time of the Temple's destruction, can fulfill all the commandments — which is not so if they do not engage in Torah, for surely they will not do all the commandments." "<b>Further</b>, it intends by saying the word <b>li</b> (\"to Me\") to awaken [the understanding] that stopping one's ears against words of Torah is like one to whom the Master of the World, the King, comes to speak and he refuses to hear Him — and this is what it says: <b>ve'im lo tishme'u li</b> (\"and if you will not hearken to Me\"), meaning: to Me you do not wish to hearken." "<b>Further</b>, the verse may be explained according to the saying of our Sages of blessed memory (Alshikh on Lamentations 1) on the verse ״ואותי עזבו ותורתי״ and so forth (Jeremiah 16:11): A parable of one who grasps a harp, etc. — end of quote. This shows that even if Israel — because of our many sins — were multiplying [acts of] idol worship and committing every transgression, yet were engaged in Torah, the Temple would not have been destroyed. And this is what HaShem in His wisdom saw fit to set forth first and say <b>ve'im lo tishme'u li</b> (\"and if you will not hearken to Me\") — then it is only if you do not perform all the commandments that the punishment described in this passage will befall you. But if you do hearken, even though you do not perform all, etc. — their judgment would be like that of one who grasps a harp, etc., as stated in their words." "<b>Further</b>, it intends along this path: Because HaShem knows the thoughts of a person's heart and the strategems of the evil inclination and the manner of its entry into a person, therefore the Torah spoke against the evil inclination and began at the beginning of its seduction. Now, I have already written to you many times that since a Jew is close to HaShem, the counsel of the evil inclination cannot prevail to tell him to do evil until it distances him little by little — like stealing the young child from his father's house, where at first it draws him away little by little. So too at first the [evil] inclination will begin speaking to him: the thing that comes from HaShem is correct — all that is written in the Torah, ״מי כמוך יחוש לדברי ה׳״ (\"who is like You, attentive to the words of YHWH\"?), but as for the many fences and safeguards and branches of prohibition that the Sages added — who is to say that the Creator so commanded? And when he inclines his ear to think critically of them, [the evil inclination] will proceed to strike him further, saying to him: These commandments are too many for you — 248 positive commandments and their branches! And once the person is somewhat distanced from HaShem, [the evil inclination] is able to strike him." "<b>And corresponding</b> to these two seductions it said <b>ve'im lo tishme'u li</b> (\"and if you will not hearken to Me\") — here it speaks of accepting the words of the Sages, following what it says (Deuteronomy 17:12): ״והאישׁ אשר יעשה בזדון לבלתי שמע וגו׳ ומת האישׁ ההוא״ (\"and the man who acts willfully, so as not to hearken, etc. — that man shall die\"). And it was precise in saying ״li״ (\"to Me\"), following the principle that whoever questions [the authority of] his rabbi is as though he questions the Shekhinah — therefore it says (Sanhedrin 110a) [of one who questions his rabbi]: ״ve'im lo tishme'u״ — you should know that the matter touches upon ״li״ (\"Me\"). And further, the Sages are the sanctuary [heikhal] of HaShem — they are He." "<b>And corresponding</b> to the second seduction, by which [the evil inclination] magnifies in his eyes the yoke of positive commandments because they are many, it said <b>ve'lo ta'asu et kol ha-mitzvot ha-eleh</b> (\"and you do not perform all these commandments\"). It also intends to say that whoever does not believe in the words of our Sages will certainly not perform even one of all the commandments, for the order of the commandments and their truth is that which the early ones established, and one who refuses to hearken to them does not have in his hand even one of all the commandments of HaShem. And therefore it did not say ״ve'im lo ta'asu״ (\"and if you do not perform\") in the same form it used in the verse that follows this one, because this [phrase] is a consequence of ״im lo tishme'u״ (\"if you do not hearken\")."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {6 "The Alshikh's parable of one who grasps a harp (תופש כינור) alludes to the Talmudic teaching (Gittin 68b, Eikhah Rabbah 1) that even serving idols, the people were still praised for occupying themselves with Torah, like a harp that still plays even if some strings are broken."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:02:28.430072242Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}